Minato's patch to frameworks/base's graphics/java/android/graphics/HardwareRenderer.java at android-15.0.0_r1
(Apache-2.0), the Unity test APK probe (PixelCopy, Unity's pause path): the process initializer asks the activity manager
neither to schedule the render thread nor whether the device has high-end graphics.

Why: framework.jar takes HardwareRenderer for PixelCopy, whose listeners extend its CopyRequest, and Minato's
IActivityManager and ActivityManager have neither setRenderThread nor isHighEndGfx, so the class would not compile.
Neither call runs on Minato: setRenderThread came after nGetRenderThreadTid, a native libandroid_runtime does not
register, and initUsingContext returns at once without the context that only ActivityThread's
HardwareRenderer.setContextForInit gives, which Minato's does not call. The high-end answer falls back to the low-RAM
one, which isHighEndGfx starts with.

--- a/graphics/java/android/graphics/HardwareRenderer.java
+++ b/graphics/java/android/graphics/HardwareRenderer.java
@@ -1324,8 +1324,7 @@
 
         private void initSched(long renderProxy) {
             try {
-                int tid = nGetRenderThreadTid(renderProxy);
-                ActivityManager.getService().setRenderThread(tid);
+                nGetRenderThreadTid(renderProxy);
             } catch (Throwable t) {
                 Log.w(LOG_TAG, "Failed to set scheduler for RenderThread", t);
             }
@@ -1349,7 +1348,7 @@
 
             initDisplayInfo();
 
-            nSetIsHighEndGfx(ActivityManager.isHighEndGfx());
+            nSetIsHighEndGfx(!ActivityManager.isLowRamDeviceStatic());
             nSetIsLowRam(ActivityManager.isLowRamDeviceStatic());
             // Defensively clear out the context in case we were passed a context that can leak
             // if we live longer than it, e.g. an activity context.
